@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Pages
@implements IHandleEvent
@using SpaceSails.Core
@*
    #242 · THE "WHY AREN'T WE MOVING?" LINE, ON ITS OWN FILE.

    Like ConditionsStrip beside it this surface is new rather than cut out of NavHud.razor, and it is fenced
    and shaped like the ones that were, because the composition (#251) splices every file in this directory
    back into the HUD before a source guard reads it.

    IHandleEvent repeats NavHud's own override, which repeats Map.Sim.cs's. The .razor.css beside this file
    carries no rules; it exists to give the component Map.razor.css's scope (pinned in the csproj).
*@
@{
@* ── MARKUP BEGINS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
                @* #242 · THE FRAME IS EATING YOUR CRUISE. Owner, post-undock: "I was wondering why the ship
                   don't move... the origin was set to Mars... after setting to Sun we got going. We should
                   have some tip about that in burn planning."

                   The sim was right and the picture was lying by omission: in the Mars frame the map
                   subtracts the heliocentric motion the ship SHARES with Mars, so a ship cruising at 33 km/s
                   and separating from Mars at 3 looks parked. Third of the frame-honesty family (#206 the
                   every-body origins, #209 the frame-cropped ribbon).

                   NOTHING NEW IS BEING TOLD. Both numbers are already on the glass — the frame chip row
                   prints "v rel Mars: 5.9" and, in the Sun frame, "v helio: 33.5". This line only says that
                   those two numbers are ABOUT each other, at the one moment it matters, which is why it
                   lives in the Plot panel (the owner's "in burn planning") and not in a banner.

                   WHEN it speaks and WHAT it says are both FrameMotionTip's, in Core: the owner's 15 % lives
                   there once, and so does the sentence, so the guard that proves the line cannot be reading
                   a threshold of its own. The row shows its working — the two speeds that made the case —
                   rather than asking to be believed.

                   THE CHIP IS ONE PRESS AND IT IS NOT A POP-UP. Nothing here can block the panel or need
                   dismissing (the 2026-08-24 ruling): it is a line in the flow with a small button on it,
                   and pressing it does exactly what the owner did by hand — SetPlotFrame(null), the Sun. *@
                @if (FrameMotionTipLine() is { } frameTip)
                {
                    <div class="map-frame-tip d-flex flex-wrap align-items-center gap-1 mb-1 text-info">
                        <span>@frameTip.Line</span>
                        <span class="text-secondary">@frameTip.Readout</span>
                        <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-info py-0"
                                title="@FrameMotionTip.SwitchChipTitle"
                                @onclick="() => SetPlotFrame(null)">@FrameMotionTip.SwitchChip</button>
                    </div>
                }
@* ── MARKUP ENDS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
}
